- FORMAT AND PRIVACY: The strict `CredentialRecordV1` decoder enforces magic/version, exact reference and generation, bounds, trailing-byte rejection, optional-passphrase state, and constant-time digest comparison before an injected format registry constructs the external signer. Stable redacted errors distinguish not-provisioned, access-denied, wrong-class, generation, malformed, passphrase, unsupported-key, and cancellation outcomes. The accepted downstream key-format owner has not assigned production format IDs, so the default registry deliberately fails closed while imported/generated fixture registries exercise the candidate-neutral libssh2 seam.
- LIFETIME: Mutable record/key/passphrase/digest buffers are cleared best-effort in bounded scopes, Security-call cancellation discards and clears returned data, and libssh2 retires the parsed credential after authentication or cancellation. Immutable Swift, Security-framework, allocator, and parser copies remain outside any provable zeroization guarantee.
- VERIFICATION: Eleven focused resolver tests include a real two-throwaway-Keychain positive/negative search-list boundary with placeholder-only bytes; 56 combined resolver/SSH/provider tests and the complete 403-test/33-suite run pass. `make validate-core`, recursive Swift format lint, core boundaries, static forbidden-surface scan, build, and diff checks all exit 0. The required deprecated file-based `SecKeychain` API emits SDK deprecation warnings but is the accepted daemon/system-domain mechanism.
- BOARD ANOMALY: Final `task-board validate` exits 0 but reports `STORY-260715-2wjwuf` stored as backlog while its child aggregate is development. The task lifecycle mutation is valid; the parent mismatch is recorded without an out-of-scope direct parent rewrite.

### 1736 — macOS provider captures one complete non-secret SSH profile generation (TASK-260715-3f4lxy)
- IMPLEMENTATION: `SSHProfileSnapshotV1` now carries the accepted complete profile, canonical endpoint, credential reference/generation, ordered host-key policy, and trust history as immutable value types. A strict deterministic JSON pass enforces UTF-8, sorted keys, canonical strings/numbers, no insignificant whitespace, duplicate rejection, depth 8, exact v1 envelope headers, recursive prohibited-field names, and exhaustive field invariants before exposing the model.

